{"tag":"RPYB","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>Find a preference relation on a countable set for which no integer-valued utility function exists.</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Proved: such an example exists. One example of this kind is the set of rationals with its usual order: between <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"0\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"1\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> lie infinitely many rationals, more than the finitely many integers strictly between <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"u(0)\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"u(1)\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span></span>.</span></p></div></div>"}